# Line endings, fixed here so that a clone gives the same bytes on any machine.
#
# This matters more than usual: the files under src/ are compiled into the game as they are, and
# the patches have to match the bytes of the tree they are applied to. Leaving it to each
# machine's core.autocrlf would make "did the recipe reproduce the tree?" depend on who cloned it.

*               text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell is happier with CRLF on Windows, and these scripts only ever run there.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# cmd.exe splits a .bat saved with LF in the wrong places: CRLF, always.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must be LF, or the shebang line carries a stray \r and the script will not run.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Patches are NOT normalised here, and this is deliberate.
#
# Upstream is mostly LF but not entirely: three lines of src/main.c in v0.3.1 end in CRLF, and
# 01-game-src.patch is the hunk that removes them. Keeping patches out of the line-ending
# machinery means a clone gets the exact bytes `git diff` produced, so whether the recipe applies
# never depends on how git was configured to rewrite it.
#
# Measured rather than assumed, on a fresh clone of v0.3.1 (docs/HOW-IT-WORKS.md, "Line
# endings"): src/main.c upstream has 3 CR and 2347 LF; after the patch it has 0 CR and 2577 LF,
# 100,854 bytes, identical to the tree the shipped package was built from. A copy of the patch
# flattened to LF also applies and produces that same file byte for byte -- git apply matches
# those three lines either way -- so this line is not what makes the recipe work. It is here so
# that it cannot stop working on a machine whose git normalises differently.
*.patch         -text
*.diff          -text

# Markdown, plain config and the manifest: LF, like everything else.
*.md            text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.appxmanifest  text eol=lf

# Images are bytes: git must neither convert them nor try to diff them.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
